 "lesson": {
  "slides": [
   {
    "id": "L1",
    "type": "mc",
    "text": "Two kinds of integral: the **indefinite** $int f(x) dx$ and the **definite** $int[a,b] f(x) dx$. Which is a number?",
    "options": [
     "the definite one",
     "the indefinite one",
     "both",
     "neither"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "hint": "Which one has limits on it?",
    "explain": "$int[a,b]$ is a number (an area). $int f(x) dx = F(x) + C$ is a family of functions."
   },
   {
    "id": "L2",
    "type": "expr",
    "text": "Find $int (x^5 + 2) dx$.",
    "answer": "x^6/6 + 2x",
    "vars": [
     "x"
    ],
    "keys": [
     "sin(",
     "cos(",
     "tan(",
     "sqrt(",
     "e^(",
     "ln("
    ],
    "label": "$int (x^5 + 2) dx$ =",
    "post": "$+ C$",
    "upToC": true,
    "show": "$x^6/6 + 2x$",
    "hint": "Term by term: $frac{x^6}{6}$ and $2x$.",
    "explain": "$frac{x^6}{6} + 2x + C$"
   },
   {
    "id": "L3",
    "type": "mc",
    "text": "Why the $+ C$?\n$$frac{d}{dx}(x^2 + 7) = 2x ;; frac{d}{dx}(x^2 - 3) = 2x$$\nSo $int 2x dx$ is",
    "options": [
     "$x^2 + C$: every one of them at once",
     "$x^2$ only",
     "$x^2 + 7$",
     "2"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "hint": "How many functions have derivative $2x$?",
    "explain": "A constant disappears when you differentiate, so the indefinite integral has to allow any constant."
   },
   {
    "id": "L4",
    "type": "expr",
    "text": "Constants and sums come along for the ride, just as with derivatives. Find\n$$int (6x^2 - 4sin(x)) dx$$",
    "answer": "2x^3 + 4cos(x)",
    "vars": [
     "x"
    ],
    "keys": [
     "sin(",
     "cos(",
     "tan(",
     "sqrt(",
     "e^(",
     "ln("
    ],
    "label": "$int (6x^2 - 4sin(x)) dx$ =",
    "show": "$2x^3 + 4cos(x)$",
    "ranges": {
     "x": [
      0.3,
      1.2
     ]
    },
    "post": "$+ C$",
    "upToC": true,
    "hint": "Term by term. An antiderivative of $sin(x)$ is $-cos(x)$.",
    "explain": "$2x^3 + 4cos(x) + C$"
   },
   {
    "id": "L5",
    "type": "mc",
    "text": "You can always check an integral by differentiating the answer. A student writes\n$$int x cos(x) dx = frac{x^2}{2} sin(x) + C$$\nDifferentiate the right side. Is the student correct?",
    "options": [
     "No. The product rule gives $x sin(x) + frac{x^2}{2} cos(x)$, not $x cos(x)$",
     "Yes"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "fixed": true,
    "hint": "The right side is a product.",
    "explain": "There is no product rule for integrals: you cannot integrate each factor separately. When in doubt, differentiate your answer."
   }
  ]
 }
}
